// rustc nll/issue-52059-report-when-borrow-and-drop-conflict.rs — upstream E0713
// imported batch B167 @ da5114692c9ebe46b869488c5f34f92eb10b98c1 (rust 1.100.0)
// ⚠ MOVED FROM THE ADMIT SHELF TO THE FAIL SHELF 2026-09-08, AND **NOT** FOR THE
//   DEFECT IT WAS ROWED FOR. The E0509 landing of that day refuses this program:
//   `fn f(x: DropStruct) -> &mut T { return x.field; }` moves a sub-value out of
//   a value that implements `Drop`, which rustc ALSO refuses as E0509, before
//   E0713 is ever reached. So the refusal is CORRECT and the program is
//   genuinely illegal — but the row's own root was the E0713 hole (a `&mut`
//   handed out PAST a destructor that then writes through it), and THAT HOLE IS
//   NOT CLOSED. It is now invisible here, because this program can no longer
//   reach it. Re-rowing it needs a program that gets the `&mut` out WITHOUT
//   moving a field out of a Drop value; filed in unrowed_backlog.ledger as
//   `e0713_mutref_past_destructor_needs_a_new_carrier`.
//   THIS WAS NOT PREDICTED. The prediction file for the E0509 half named 7 rows
//   and these two were not among them; they closed in the correct direction and
//   are reported as an unpredicted closing, not folded into the count.
// shelf: admit · root nllmoves.E
// RE-PORTED AS-IS 2026-09-07 (stage 5). The previous port carried only upstream's
// `finish_1`. All four of upstream's constructs were restored and compiled, and
// THE ROW SPLITS: `finish_1` (return the `&mut` field directly, E0713) and
// `finish_4` (move the field out of a `Drop` type, E0509) are ADMITTED and stay
// here; `finish_2` (`&mut *s.url` through a local) and `finish_3` (the same with
// a type annotation) are REFUSED today — with the WRONG SENTENCE:
//     error [fn finish_2]: cannot return reference to local variable 'p': dangling reference
// upstream's reason is "borrow may still be in use when destructor runs", and `p`
// is a reference, not a local whose address escapes. The verdict is right and the
// sentence is not, so they are NOT landed on the fail shelf here (that would pin a
// wrong sentence as coverage) and they are not left in this file either (the file
// must compile silently for its row to mean anything). Control, measured
// 2026-09-07: `fn passthru(x: &mut i64) -> &mut i64 { let p: &mut i64 = x; return p; }`
// compiles clean, so the refusal is specific to the field of the by-value `S`,
// not a general over-refusal of reference passthrough.
// Original path: tests/ui/nll/issue-52059-report-when-borrow-and-drop-conflict.rs
// upstream commit: 4b0c9d76ae7d387229caea55cfa73c280b08b8a7
// upstream construct: lines 10-13 —
//     fn finish_1(s: S) -> &mut String { s.url }
//     //~^^ ERROR borrow may still be in use when destructor runs
// Modifications:
//   · `String` -> `i64` (the payload is scenery; `S` is the `Drop` type under test)
//   · the explicit `<'a>` on `S` and its `Drop` impl -> elided (this port needs no
//     second lifetime to name)
//   · the tail expressions are `return`s
//   · assert -> exit code: `fn main() -> i32`
//   · finish_2 / finish_3 are held out — see the split above
//   · finish_4 is held out for a DIFFERENT reason: it is upstream's E0509 (move out
//     of a type that implements `Drop`), which is a BLESSED DIVERGENCE here —
//     docs/spec/expressions.md `intrinsic.drop.skip-moved-out-paths`. It is admitted,
//     and correctly so; keeping it in this file would make part of the row's
//     admission licensed, which is exactly the confusion this stage exists to remove.
package issue_52059_report_when_borrow_and_drop_conflict;
struct S { url: &mut i64 }
impl Drop for S { fn drop(self: &mut S) { } }
fn finish_1(s: S) -> &mut i64 { return s.url; }            // THE DEFECT: admitted, upstream E0713
fn main() -> i32 { return 0i32; }
